[Music]  sometimes in a search for meaning  you could find yourself browsing in the  philosophy section of a bookstore  it's here where you might come across an  idea that changes your life  in kevin eulog's case he discovered an  idea that changed a lot of lives this  book really convinced me that i can and  should like try to make a big positive  impact  kevin is a 32 year old software  developer living in ottawa back in 2014  he came across this book by australian  philosopher peter singer  it paints a very emotional picture of  the plate of the extremely poor and then  it's like very concretely like here's  what you can do that will help them then  i became convinced that it was like  literally the correct thing to do like  this is the action that i should take  kevin now donates 25  of his income to charity for context the  average canadian gives less than two  percent but he's not donating this large  sum to just any charity kevin is part of  a growing global movement that uses  evidence and reason to figure out the  best way to do good in the world it's  called effective altruism  even though kevin had never consistently  donated to charity before he found  himself compelled to give  effective altruism introduced me to the  idea that like i can work my totally  normal job and still like give  ambitiously and make like a significant  impact  if we're going to live an ethical life  it's not enough just to not cheat steal  mime kill but that if we have enough we  have to share some of that with people  who have so little  that's peter singer  to make this point he uses an analogy  so  i ask you to imagine that one day you're  walking across a park  and in this park there's a pond  but you look at it and you do notice  that there is something in the pond  splashing around  and when you look closer you're shocked  to see that it's actually a very small  child  and the child seems to be drowning  you rush down to the pond as you do the  thought occurs to you damn i've just put  on my most expensive shoes because i'm  going to this important occasion  is this child really my responsibility  and he says most will agree you ought to  save the child even if it means you'll  ruin  your clothing anthony skelton teaches  moral philosophy at western university  if you can prevent something very bad  from happening like the loss of a life  without sacrificing anything of  comparable moral importance like your  own life  then you ought morally speaking to do it  he says you're in a similar relationship  to the global poor as you were to that  young child  effective altruism combines this notion  with the idea that one charity can be 10  to 100 times more effective than another  so you should give to charities that do  the most good for the most people so  it's really about sort of emphasizing  the sort of common adage of you know you  want to get the most bang for your buck  kevin started out by giving a few  hundred dollars to charities that give  out bed nets to prevent the spread of  malaria  now he donates tens of thousands of  dollars to organizations that prepare  for extreme famine and the next pandemic  causes considered high impact by  organizations like givewell  which evaluates and ranks charities  based on how many lives are saved  25 of your income is not insignificant  how has that impacted you and your your  lifestyle the main thing that i did to  increase my giving was that i  intentionally moved out of my one  bedroom apartment and moved in with  roommates so that i could have more  money to donate  so this is my room  in addition to downsizing he also became  a vegan animal welfare is another high  priority cause that effective altruists  care about  there's actually a method for how they  decide where to focus their efforts it's  called the itn framework which stands  for importance tractability and  neglectedness  so they just want to know is there a  neglected issue where they can produce a  lot of good and where we think if we do  take concrete steps we can really change  the effective altruism movement has  thousands of members worldwide including  around 400 in canada who have donated  hundreds of millions of dollars to  charities  the movement attracts everyone from  students to billionaires all of whom  pledged to give away at least 10 percent  of their income over their lives but  effective altruism isn't just about  donating money some effective altruists  have donated kidneys to strangers while  others like grishika bose plan to  dedicate their life's work to solving  big global problems i think i found out  about effective altruism  maybe when i was 15 i was trying to  figure out what was important to me and  what i wanted to do  just in general  initially rushika thought she might go  into physics because it's a subject she  enjoyed but then she discovered the  website 80 000 hours co-founder benjamin  todd encourages students to take high  impact careers rather than simply  following their passion  even if you match your passion with your  work and you're successful you can still  quite easily fail to have a fulfilling  career that's because you might not find  the work meaningful  that made me start thinking about do i  really just want to go into career doing  research in physics pursuing some  abstract  ideas that might not have any  application  will that  make me happy  or  is there something i could do where i  could make a difference  rashika is now finishing her degree in  cognitive systems at the university of  british columbia  she hopes to pursue a career in  artificial intelligence risk a field  popular among effective altruists and  once she gets a job she plans to donate  a portion of her earnings to effective  charities  i've always been brought up to try to  help people and to prioritize trying to  make the world a better place at least  in a small way  my mother is a doctor a pediatrician  she would always try to help the  children  around us in india when i found out  about ea which is not only about  altruism but about  effective altruism about trying to do  good as efficiently as we can  it  drew me in because  it just seemed to make so much sense  young people in cities and on university  campuses are attracted to effective  altruism  even as research shows younger canadians  are donating less than their parents did  at their age i wanted to speak to  someone about this so i reached out to  the person who started it all  peter singer  do you see effective altruism  as  a thing that's going to save the charity  sector by getting you know millennials  and gen zers involved in giving and  making that a part of their daily life  they don't want to go on  in traditional ways of giving charity  where essentially you hand them a  regular donation and you don't really  ask them what they're doing with it so  i think that is something that appeals  to younger people and that if we can get  the effective altruism message out more  widely we  will do something to restore  interest in charitable giving  you've advocated for various causes over  the course of your career animal rights  as well as global poverty  how is effective altruism given your  life meaning  i suppose i grew up with a with a sense  that it's really bad for them to be  suffering you know extreme suffering is  something that is really terrible i mean  that may be my family background because  my family went through the holocaust  some of them survived some didn't when  you can reduce that suffering and make a  difference  then you feel that you've lived your  life well but that you've done something  worthwhile and that's what  effective altruism has done for  me roshika is starting an effective  altruism club on campus and she says  there's already been a lot of interest  we're always told about all the problems  in the world  we're bombarded with news about it every  day as a teenager through social media  and this just feels like a way we can  really  take the problems head on  and feel like we're actually doing  something  and so when you find out that you can  like save an individual human life  uh that's like that's that's a lot it's  a big source of meaning for me now  the ideas of effective altruism seem at  odds with our culture today which is  more interested in the pursuit of  individual happiness and success  than in the search for meaning  the movement is perhaps a reaction to  that  a way for each and every one of us to  make the world a better place  one life at a time

